{
  "summary": "P1 credential compromise (KB-029). Reporter: Aisha Khan (user_id 5, HR Generalist, America/New_York, device BL-LT-0105). She clicked the link in a \"payroll verification\" email and typed her password into the page. Time of click not given in ticket; ticket received ~2026-10-10/11. Only password reported entered; no Duo push approval or attachment mentioned. Checks: sign-in logs (14 days) show 10 successful sign-ins, all New York US, corporate/residential IPs (203.0.113.10, 198.51.100.25), all within usual hours with Duo push, last at 2026-10-09 13:35 UTC. No hosting networks, no foreign sign-ins, no failed or denied MFA. No suspicious activity seen yet, so block_sign_in was NOT requested. Duo enrolled (Duo Mobile on her phone). Quarantine has a matching phishing message Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain, capital I instead of l), subject \"Urgent: verify your payroll login\", received 2026-10-10 21:04 UTC, marked phishing and not releasable. The copy she clicked probably got delivered to her inbox or somewhere else, so look for other copies in her mailbox and across the org. Actions: reset_password queued (approval id 1). The technician must verify her by callback and give the temp password by phone. Still needed: approve the reset, keep watching sign-ins for attacker use, block the sender domain brightIine-help.example tenant-wide, and check whether other users got or clicked the same message.",
  "reason": "KB-029 requires escalation every time; P1 credential compromise"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Aisha, thank you for telling us right away. Reporting quickly makes a big difference. We're treating your password as stolen. Here's what's happening and what you can do:\n\n1. We've asked for your password to be reset. Once a technician approves it, they will call the number we have on file to confirm it's you, then give you a temporary password over the phone. We will never send it by email. You'll set a new password the next time you sign in. Don't reuse the old one anywhere, and if you used it on any other site, change it there too.\n2. Please don't delete the email. Security will want to look at it. Don't click it again or forward it to anyone.\n3. If you get a Duo push you didn't start, deny it and tell us right away. Never approve a push you didn't start.\n\nWe checked recent sign-ins on your account and saw nothing unusual so far. Your security team has the case and will keep watching it.",
  "internal_note": "P1 under KB-029. The user entered her password on a payroll-verification phishing page. Time of click unknown. No Duo approval or attachment reported. Sign-in logs: all 10 sign-ins in the last 14 days are from New York on corporate/residential IPs, in usual hours, with Duo. Latest was 2026-10-09 13:35 UTC. No suspicious activity, so I did not request block_sign_in. Duo is enrolled. Quarantine has the matching phishing message Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain), 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', marked not releasable. It was not released. The copy she clicked likely reached her inbox. Password reset is queued as approval #1: verify her by callback and give the temp password by phone only. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation #1) to keep monitoring, block the sender domain, and search the org for other copies.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-029",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
